{"title":"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Remark_and_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark and Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_March_2026)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remarks (added by pf02, March 2026)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Remark_about_Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark about Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be a given number greater than 2. We consider the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> of all the integers of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/e/8de86497cfc157ecf7759d1c94d31c8b3ba23ea2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + kn$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/2/a6250e1c2ace5b7a875c446d32d372a91867a1f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1, 2, \\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" /> A number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> is called indecomposable in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> if there are not two numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/5/d457bcb364e21cf3fa0b23afb4ee0aa637354c9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m = pq.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"11\" /> Prove that there exist a number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/d/fadabce9d11979e64e9a4bf5eb2d5d121f2206b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r \\in V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> that can be expressed as the product of elements indecomposable in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> in more than one way. (Expressions which differ only in order of the elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> will be considered the same.)\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Lemma: there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/7/b671f1bb7e4ee86584347d5d22f1dc8abdb5bef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"9\" /> many prime numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/2/6a2e9da3f9073843ce5f66c686d9c524afa221af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\not\\equiv 1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Proof:\n</p><p>Assume that there are only finitely many of them and let their product be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\nThen all prime factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/a/4ba248276bf24ebedd9432d0b6e1db167b0e29e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nk - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" /> must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/e/4ae99a3c4a324063c258a5864d3ecd388439fee2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\equiv 1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"92\" height=\"13\" /> since this number is coprime with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. But that would mean that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/a/c6af6ea5890302a989bc01463b1d4aa089165c4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$- 1 \\equiv nk - 1 \\equiv 1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"196\" height=\"13\" /> which is impossible for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/7/097569af11a8c230c95e6550101ff0b0e7ac7958.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &gt; 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>Now we can tackle the problem:\nThere are only finetely many residue classes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/4/2840619da2b7d127bb9e44b95f421d608e8732a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mod n$\" width=\"63\" height=\"12\" />, thus we can find two primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/7/f779d95c15f7e41de74cb767f52fe4e7b92c745e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p,q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/7/d472963df1902f26b4b45a06a3866ba6b46018b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\equiv q \\not\\equiv 1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"140\" height=\"17\" /> and coprime with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> be the smallest positive integer with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/2/77290ce9815eaa7863c0c51a02a79ae47cea1efe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^s \\equiv 1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"114\" height=\"16\" />, then the same property holds for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> too.\nNow we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df1b42e5032a3e26fa25b49dbe4dfa27c0b9cf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^s, q^s, pq^{s - 1} , p^{s - 1}q \\equiv 1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"238\" height=\"18\" /> are all indecomposable since all their nontrivial divisors are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/e/e1e78f758fd2129bf771069b848a3e53aecf9a6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\not\\equiv 1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"17\" />. But the product <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/4/ee43d5204003c3ff48d65f14a63ec0fa8c83617b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^sq^s = (pq^{s - 1})(p^{s - 1}q)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"168\" height=\"19\" /> gives a number that is represented in two ways.\n</p><p>The above solution was posted and copyrighted by ZetaX.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark_and_Solution_2\">Remark and Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Remark and Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The lemma is incorrect. A simple example: k=5, n=3, 2 is a prime factor of nk-1=14 but 2≡2 (not 1) mod n.\n</p><p>Following is my approach.\nWe build <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/c/19ca9a51e9ce716dfb6ed7f6b469d351f1ad5ce5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=abcd$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"70\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/7/f17c4024921468e20f8f5233864eeb2b8ba8f3cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab \\equiv cd \\equiv ac \\equiv bd \\equiv 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"177\" height=\"14\" /> mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba41d39350d412f0af854ef82201b63acab33a74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab,cd,ac,bd$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"96\" height=\"18\" /> are indecomposable,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/c/33c9c5b6f1ae7f857a1e706ded936f3676538b81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\neq d, b \\neq c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"93\" height=\"18\" />.\nThen <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdfc943539f3e562458db2283647f1f34a719bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=ab \\cdot cd=ac \\cdot bd$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"155\" height=\"14\" /> satisfies the requirement.\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb60e0857a2c0a2c47acfbe81781032feb1f7150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c,d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> all be written in the form of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/4/204d109f6c82afa00f61831fb444850d188d67ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$kn-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" />, so that their pairwise products <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/5/2954e98ca6e8ae6a0440448f6ccd5f57507f4d71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\equiv 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\nWe set the values of elements as small as possible to maximize the chance of being indecomposable.\nSo we set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/6/0a63e281e842da4faa94c9792929e99362a84eb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=b=n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"110\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/0/c7059eefaccd82fef9d5c5076167de90db7b3ea8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=d=2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"119\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/3/52359e65f068f7a83aeda08dc48fd80d6ee46fb2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab=(n-1)^2&lt;(n+1)(2n+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"253\" height=\"21\" />, therefore indecomposable;\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/5/d8578ebaa77ce931834c60c3a7543c5231e9099a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ac,bd=(n-1)(2n-1)&lt;(n+1)(2n+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"337\" height=\"20\" />, therefore indecomposable;\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbc60be150ed6836fead969c643dcad9261d2ac3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cd=(2n-1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"116\" height=\"21\" />, if it’s decomposable, its factors must include <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />, because the only element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744280a1131dbc09035f25e742e47d526d6e64fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" />\nsmaller than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f9a96c63183be91b9fe70e6e16dfa4bfec6f3da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />.\nThen <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/2/f5232918ef08124f485e34e4ab40acc192a57973.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1) | (4n^2-4n+1)=4n(n+1)-8n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"353\" height=\"21\" />, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/7/967e2ca8c4d87e5d4a327ca27808a87433ed111a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1) | (8n-1)=8(n+1)-9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"250\" height=\"20\" />, therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/3/1338256dec8b93234b4c2af62cc74064642493bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1) | 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"72\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/6/cc68c87b25cd9c4e6c72c86aae558a67bf2f5968.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1=1,3,9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"112\" height=\"17\" />, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/b/e1b2e49f58f14d9e07ebbf2ffdd58971096ec4f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 3, n=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"17\" />, meaning if\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/b/8fbabb6295ffdcaf009220fdae68e9a7f0ff3ef5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\neq 8, (2n-1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"21\" /> is indecomposable;\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/5/e253fda94bba8dfaf7f8efd0e47fe72a9f5d4525.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\neq 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" />, set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/a/59a8de3d6653be36cbfc06e00d822a78a2b161f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=b=n-1, c=d=2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"235\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdfc943539f3e562458db2283647f1f34a719bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=ab \\cdot cd=ac \\cdot bd$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"155\" height=\"14\" />, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/8/288a8e48d9d546b6374317f0ca5bc7827728ef82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ab=(n-2)n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"142\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/2/532bba46bc67e48441d3cd9c86ef676278e351dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ac=bd=(2n-3)n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"193\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/9/5491efd0003c0abf01801e375953f16aaff728ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cd=(4n-4)n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"151\" height=\"20\" />, all conforming to the form of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/6/5b665c53cb60022a8ac4c800bcf85a96eadec0df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$kn+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" />;\nAs aforementioned, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/0/57099b9ebbc3370d06c4a71ca3ffca58c570ddea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-1)^2, (n-1)(2n-1), (2n-1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"277\" height=\"21\" /> are now all indecomposable, and obviously\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94c779afeea0a1abeb6704ed17dd08bcc875294e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n-1)^2 \\neq (n-1)(2n-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"212\" height=\"21\" />, valid;\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/4/1d4f171162b090bab7ce5dfa1c58715850530ba5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=8$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, we can take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/1/a21bf03b998fff33d105264f26f78c30b99815ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=b=n-1=7, c=d=3n-1=23$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"311\" height=\"18\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/d/62d78a44226c5267efd2c1d62cca3a732bc5da6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=49 \\cdot 529=161 \\cdot 161=25921$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"255\" height=\"14\" />, the first few elements are: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/8/b28a37b938420a4c99758e4122e7c113a4143888.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1=9, 2n+1=17, 3n+1=25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"281\" height=\"17\" />,\nand the only prime factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/f/a3f79a452ad0d353b3895633acf75aeb8251eff7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49, 161, 529$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"17\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />3, therefore they are indecomposable,\nvalid.\n</p><p>Proof completed.\n</p><p>This contribution is from Tmath2024\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remarks_.28added_by_pf02.2C_March_2026.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_March_2026)\">Remarks (added by pf02, March 2026)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remarks (added by pf02, March 2026)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/a/12a13743f0019a51abbaa321af540802715548bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />: The remark in the first sentence of Solution 2 (saying that\n\"The lemma is incorrect...\", referring to the Lemma in the first Solution)\nis wrong. There is nothing wrong with the Lemma, it is correct.\n</p><p>Also, I made some editing of Solution 2 (for readability), and I corrected\none typo.\n</p><p>Solution 2 is badly incomplete (in addition to being so sloppily written);\nit relies too heavily on hand-waving. The shortcomings are in the fact that\nin order to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/0/de0a3f63a3dd478d69b819e2597873c2b1b790a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n - 1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/3/9c37648fa6ba131e1fc8add1a707330600bceb74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n - 1)(2n - 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"124\" height=\"20\" /> are indecomposable,\nit is not enough to compare them to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/2/a7207a5fc4ac3c4a3c8293a8eb89654f27915e2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n + 1)(2n + 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"124\" height=\"20\" /> (or, it is not obvious\nwhy this would be enough). Also, for showing that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c2838fe3bd59e8415ba16dbb7bfb2bebe6e7c9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n - 1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"21\" /> is indecomposable,\nit is not enough to only consider the case when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/5/ac5429dc4aa7ba1f3b20be4599a34c4220cc79db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> is one of its factors.\nThese shortcomings can be fixed by a diligent reader, and I will not complete\nthis proof.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/9/f4974d7b088479a8eb32891d86d0b46aedb5c363.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{2}$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" />: Following this remark, I transcribe a solution from the discussion\npage <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://aops.com/community/p397316\">https://aops.com/community/p397316</a> by Taha1381.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/d/38d349451f157180bef169527de7d4499465377c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />: After Solution 3, I will add a remark which simplifies the solution.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Dirichlet%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Dirichlet&#39;s Theorem\">Dirichlet's Theorem</a> (see also\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet%27s_theorem_on_arithmetic_progressions\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet%27s_theorem_on_arithmetic_progressions</a>)\nthere are infinity many primes congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. Take two of them,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/e/75e83f9ab9dfbf8453c11434327dd45cd61cb5f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\neq q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/0/b505fd0298cb55c54a3aff36d9ad01b0d91ee3cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(pq)(pq) = (p^2)(q^2) = p^2q^2 \\in V_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"250\" height=\"21\" />. Obviously\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/1/501c2d76823f160cfc4b42683e3bc7c7614c6a3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$pq, p^2, q^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"20\" /> are indecomposable, so we are done.\n</p><p>This is a rewriting of the solution by Taha1381 from the discussion page\n(original thread) mentioned below.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark_about_Solution_3\">Remark about Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Remark about Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We don't need the full force of Dirichlet's theorem.  All we need is to\nshow that there are infinitely many primes congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/e/bae5aba07d37ff6ff813107e76260fb31ad5794e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n(Dirichlet's theorem tells us much more: there is an infinite arithmetic\nprogression of such primes). This can be shown with a proof similar to\nthe proof of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euclid%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Euclid&#39;s Theorem\">Euclid's Theorem</a> about the infiniteness of primes:\n</p><p>Assume that there would be only finitely many primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/6522c24090a827c9a0b5b459213592940ee42c08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\equiv -1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"122\" height=\"18\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d27a542f9d839607c3303841fce3d5ae2d7f213a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1, \\dots, p_r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"13\" /> be all these primes.\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/3/2a3f82e287e894689df5857fb1c31efa4bdc3c93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = p_1 \\dots p_r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"96\" height=\"18\" />. Now let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be a prime factor of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/0/8707ccc419d421a129db7f2a01c0ac0bb195fc37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$kn - 1 = n p_1 \\dots p_r - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"181\" height=\"18\" />. So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/3/cb31246e1f17cf6733a0c2c1188d453f8de5d98c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n p_1 \\dots p_r - 1 = qa$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"17\" />\nfor some number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. On the one hand, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd1309a577cbc459c44471ef5bf02ac0da477988.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q \\neq p_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"51\" height=\"19\" /> by construction.\nOn the other hand, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/6/d0658625a4d162f9003be630341f8c710b7fdb40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q \\equiv -1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"122\" height=\"18\" /> by construction. This is a\ncontradiction, because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d27a542f9d839607c3303841fce3d5ae2d7f213a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1, \\dots, p_r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"13\" /> were all the primes\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/a/a1a47a1ee9de593110a88c17d06d4115f4bd389e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\equiv -1 \\mod n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"108\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nThe original thread for this problem can be found here: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://aops.com/community/p397316\">[1]</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO\" title=\"1977 IMO\">1977 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017031124\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.042 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.062 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 517/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 895/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 18131/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.769      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.769      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:20521:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017031124 and revision id 279156. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}